55
00:02:02,538 --> 00:02:04,409
This lesson is about The Residue Theorem.

56
00:02:05,303 --> 00:02:07,190
One number that solves impossible real

57
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:07,646
integrals

58
00:02:10,023 --> 00:02:10,815
Here is the plan.

59
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:12,205
The Big Idea.

60
00:02:12,545 --> 00:02:14,000
Computing Residues Fast.

61
00:02:14,469 --> 00:02:15,196
A Real Integral.

62
00:02:16,241 --> 00:02:18,344
What if a whole contour integral depended

63
00:02:18,344 --> 00:02:19,616
on just one number per point?

64
00:02:21,469 --> 00:02:23,087
Take a function that is analytic

65
00:02:23,087 --> 00:02:25,434
everywhere inside a closed loop, except at

66
00:02:25,434 --> 00:02:26,534
a few isolated poles.

67
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:29,298
Cauchy tells us the loop integral of an

68
00:02:29,298 --> 00:02:30,682
analytic function is zero.

69
00:02:31,423 --> 00:02:33,097
So all the action must come from the

70
00:02:33,097 --> 00:02:33,467
poles.

71
00:02:34,461 --> 00:02:36,736
Near each pole, the Laurent series has a

72
00:02:36,736 --> 00:02:38,085
one over z minus a term.

73
00:02:38,622 --> 00:02:40,328
Its coefficient is called the residue.

74
00:02:40,984 --> 00:02:43,093
Every other term integrates to zero around

75
00:02:43,093 --> 00:02:43,482
the loop.

76
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:45,266
Only the residue survives, and it

77
00:02:45,266 --> 00:02:47,115
contributes two pi i times itself.

78
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:50,380
You rarely need the full Laurent series to

79
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,110
find a residue.

80
00:02:52,348 --> 00:02:55,933
For a simple pole, multiply by z minus a,

81
00:02:55,933 --> 00:02:57,873
then take the limit as z goes to a.

82
00:02:59,029 --> 00:03:01,079
Find the residue of one over z squared

83
00:03:01,079 --> 00:03:02,588
plus one at z equals i.

84
00:03:03,804 --> 00:03:05,767
Here is a real integral that ordinary

85
00:03:05,767 --> 00:03:08,363
calculus finds hard, and residues make

86
00:03:08,363 --> 00:03:08,574
easy.

87
00:03:10,328 --> 00:03:12,915
Evaluate the integral of one over one plus

88
00:03:12,915 --> 00:03:15,146
x squared, over the whole real line.

89
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:18,495
Close the contour with a big semicircle in

90
00:03:18,495 --> 00:03:20,135
the upper half plane.

91
00:03:20,135 --> 00:03:22,488
Only the pole at z equals i lies inside.

92
00:03:23,548 --> 00:03:25,328
The semicircle contributes nothing as the

93
00:03:25,328 --> 00:03:27,917
radius grows, so the integral equals two

94
00:03:27,917 --> 00:03:29,374
pi i times one over two i.

95
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:31,535
Let's recap.

96
00:03:32,036 --> 00:03:33,879
We covered The Big Idea, Computing

97
00:03:33,879 --> 00:03:35,900
Residues Fast, A Real Integral.

98
00:03:36,402 --> 00:03:38,342
